In this lesson we'll look at modelling with proportion in context — basically, picking the right number format so your answer actually means what you think it means.

Here's the big idea. Real life throws numbers at you in different shapes. A survey might say twelve out of thirty students. A school report might say enrolments grew four point seven percent. A fundraiser might say we made thirty-five dollars profit on fifty dollars outlay. Each one needs a different way to report it, and that's what choosing the representation is about.

When you want exact comparisons or raw counts, fractions and decimals are great — like saying two thirds of the class. When you're comparing parts of different-sized wholes, percentages are king. Fifty-five percent of Year 7 attending the disco works whether there are four hundred students or forty. When you're comparing one group to another, use a ratio, like two girls for every three boys. And when something goes up or down, like a lift moving from floor three down five floors, use a signed number — negative two makes sense.

Then there's proportional modelling. If fifty-five percent of four hundred Year 7s attended, you just multiply zero point five five by four hundred to get two hundred twenty students. Easy. But here's the trap — always say what the whole is. Twenty-three percent of the school is meaningless unless we know how big the school is.

Now let's see it in action.